#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""bonsai — a little Treemarks.

One Mac, a personal Claude and/or ChatGPT login, and one or more institution seats (say,
Stanford's Claude and Codex). Bonsai keeps every login as its own saved CLI sign-in (a "pool"),
shows how much room each one has left and when it resets, shows this machine's load on the row
beneath, and routes a job to the login you name or to the one with the most perishable room:

    bonsai doctor                       what is installed, what is signed in
    bonsai login <email> --claude       sign a login in as its own pool (also --codex)
    bonsai login <email> --codex --institution
    bonsai gauge                        the live board (q or Ctrl-C quits); --once for one frame
    bonsai route [claude|codex]         the order a job would take, and why
    bonsai run --pool <name> -- <cmd>   run any command under that login
    bonsai job stanford-codex "prompt"  a headless job on a named seat
    bonsai job astra -f notes.md "..."  a model alias picks the provider; auto picks the login
    bonsai pass astra -f draft.md       an adversarial pass: a second model tries to break it
    bonsai jobs                         recent jobs and their outcomes
    bonsai install-skill                teach Claude Code the words ("route this to my stanford codex")

THE ONE THING TO KNOW. A job you route from a chat (Claude Code, Codex) to an institution seat
goes THROUGH that chat: the chat writes the task and reads the answer, on its own login. The seat's
protections cover the seat's call, not the chat on either side of it. Bonsai is for work that may
sit in that chat anyway. For protected data (PHI, PII, student records, anything your institution
classes Moderate or High Risk) use the institution's tools directly, as a person; Bonsai refuses a
chat-driven job that declares such data (--data moderate|high|phi) before it reads a file.

Pools live where the Treemarks fleet keeps them: ~/.claude-pools/<email> (CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR) and
~/.codex-pools/<email> (CODEX_HOME); `default` is ~/.claude and ~/.codex, the logins you use by
hand. Identity and usage come from the CLIs themselves (`claude auth status`, Codex's app-server);
Bonsai reads a stored Claude token only if you turn that on. Standard library only; macOS; Python
3.9 (the one Xcode's tools install) or newer. Windows: the platform branches exist, untested.
"""
